lesson "Intro to Real Functions" {
  course: "algebra"
  skills: ["func-domain", "func-range", "func-domain-restrictions"]

  slide "Welcome to Functions!" {
    id: "l1-intro"
    cat: "Basics"
    > Functions are the fundamental building blocks of mathematics. Think of a
    > function as a machine: you input one number, and it outputs exactly one
    > corresponding number. Our goal is to understand the two main rules:
    > **Domain** (valid inputs) and **Range** (possible outputs).
  }

  slide "The 'Plus 2' Machine" {
    id: "l1-machine"
    cat: "Basics"
    > Consider the function f(x) = x + 2. This machine takes any input and adds 2
    > to it. Drag the point to feed the machine different inputs and watch the output.

    scene plane {
      x: [-5, 10]
      y: [-5, 15]
      grid
      axes
      param t = 3 { range: [-2, 8], step: 0.1 }
      curve f = x + 2 { color: primary, width: 3 }
      line drop = (t, 0) -> (t, t + 2) { color: neutral, style: dashed }
      line over = (0, t + 2) -> (t, t + 2) { color: neutral, style: dashed }
      point p = (t, t + 2) { color: danger, r: 7, drag: x -> t, label: "f(${t}) = ${t + 2}" }
      slider t { label: "Input x" }
    }
  }

  slide "The Domain: Valid Inputs" {
    id: "l1-domain"
    cat: "Domain"
    > What numbers can we put into f(x) = x + 2? Any real number! There are no
    > restrictions. The **Domain** is all real numbers, written as (-∞, ∞).

    scene plane {
      x: [-10, 10]
      y: [-10, 10]
      grid
      axes
      param t = 2 { range: [-8, 8], step: 0.1 }
      curve f = x + 2 { color: primary, width: 3 }
      line domain = (-10, 0) -> (10, 0) { color: success, width: 4 }
      label at (7, 0.8) = "Domain" { color: success }
      point p = (t, t + 2) { color: danger, r: 6 }
      slider t { label: "Input x" }
    }
  }

  slide "The Range: Possible Outputs" {
    id: "l1-range"
    cat: "Range"
    > Since we can input any number, we can also get any number out. The **Range**
    > is also all real numbers, (-∞, ∞).

    scene plane {
      x: [-10, 10]
      y: [-10, 10]
      grid
      axes
      param t = 2 { range: [-8, 8], step: 0.1 }
      curve f = x + 2 { color: primary, width: 3 }
      line range = (0, -10) -> (0, 10) { color: accent, width: 4 }
      label at (1.4, 8) = "Range" { color: accent }
      point p = (t, t + 2) { color: danger, r: 6 }
      slider t { label: "Input x" }
    }
  }

  slide "Rule #1: Division by Zero" {
    id: "l1-div-zero"
    cat: "Restrictions"
    > Some functions have restrictions. You cannot divide by zero. Consider
    > f(x) = 1/x. As x gets closer to 0, the value explodes. At x=0, it's undefined.

    scene plane {
      x: [-5, 5]
      y: [-10, 10]
      grid
      axes
      param t = 2 { range: [-4, 4], step: 0.1 }
      curve f = 1 / x { color: danger, width: 3 }
      line asymp = (0, -10) -> (0, 10) { color: neutral, style: dashed }
      label at (0.6, 8) = "x≠0" { color: neutral }
      point p = (t, 1 / t) { color: primary, r: 6 }
      slider t { label: "Input x" }
    }
  }

  slide "Quiz: Identify the Restriction" {
    id: "l1-quiz-1"
    cat: "Assessment"
    quiz {
      ask "For the function f(x) = 5 / (x - 3), which input value is not allowed?"
      - "All real numbers"
      * "All real numbers except x = 3"
      - "All real numbers except x = 5"
      - "x > 3"
    }
  }

  slide "Rule #2: Negative Square Roots" {
    id: "l1-sqrt"
    cat: "Restrictions"
    > In the real number system, you cannot take the square root of a negative
    > number. Try f(x) = √x. The graph only exists for x ≥ 0.

    scene plane {
      x: [-2, 10]
      y: [-2, 5]
      grid
      axes
      param t = 4 { range: [0, 9], step: 0.1 }
      curve f = sqrt(x) { color: success, width: 3 }
      point p = (t, sqrt(t)) { color: danger, r: 6 }
      label at (-1, 1) = "No Graph Here" { color: neutral }
      slider t { label: "Input x" }
    }
  }

  slide "Challenge: Find the Domain" {
    id: "l1-quiz-2"
    cat: "Assessment"
    quiz {
      ask "For f(x) = √(x - 2), what is the smallest valid x ?"
      - "(-∞, 2)"
      - "(-∞, 2]"
      - "(2, ∞)"
      * "[2, ∞)"
    }
  }
}
